{
 "id": "degree-radian-conversion",
 "title": "Exact degree–radian conversion",
 "summary": "Convert between degrees and radians exactly, keeping π in the answer.",
 "objectives": [
  "Explain what one radian measures",
  "Convert degrees to radians in simplest form",
  "Convert radians to degrees"
 ],
 "prerequisites": [],
 "lesson": [
  {
   "id": "drc-b01",
   "type": "text",
   "md": "# Degrees and radians\n\nA **radian** is the central angle that cuts off an arc **as long as the radius**. A full turn is a whole circumference, $2\\pi r$, so it contains $2\\pi$ radians. That gives the one conversion you need: $360^\\circ=2\\pi$, or more simply $180^\\circ=\\pi$."
  },
  {
   "id": "drc-b02",
   "type": "math",
   "tex": "180^\\circ=\\pi\\ \\text{rad}\\qquad 1^\\circ=\\frac{\\pi}{180}\\ \\text{rad}\\qquad 1\\ \\text{rad}=\\frac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}\\approx 57.3^\\circ",
   "label": "The one conversion you need"
  },
  {
   "id": "drc-b03",
   "type": "numberline",
   "min": 0,
   "max": 6.2832,
   "pi": true,
   "points": [
    {
     "value": "pi/6",
     "label": "30°"
    },
    {
     "value": "pi/2",
     "label": "90°"
    },
    {
     "value": "pi",
     "label": "180°"
    },
    {
     "value": "3*pi/2",
     "label": "270°"
    },
    {
     "value": "2*pi",
     "label": "360°"
    }
   ],
   "caption": "Radians on a number line: $\\pi$ is half a turn."
  },
  {
   "id": "drc-b04",
   "type": "predict",
   "question": "Without a calculator: is $3$ radians more or less than $180^\\circ$?",
   "options": [
    {
     "md": "More",
     "correct": false,
     "why": "$180^\\circ$ is $\\pi\\approx3.14$ radians, which is a bit **more** than $3$."
    },
    {
     "md": "Less",
     "correct": true,
     "why": "$\\pi\\approx 3.14>3$, so $3$ radians is a little less than $180^\\circ$: about $172^\\circ$."
    },
    {
     "md": "Exactly $180^\\circ$",
     "correct": false,
     "why": "That would be $\\pi$ radians, not $3$."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "drc-b05",
   "type": "steps",
   "title": "Degrees to radians, exactly",
   "steps": [
    {
     "title": "Multiply",
     "md": "Multiply by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ}$ so the degrees cancel."
    },
    {
     "title": "Simplify",
     "md": "Reduce the fraction and keep $\\pi$ as a symbol."
    },
    {
     "title": "Check",
     "md": "Sanity check with a known angle: $90^\\circ$ must give $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "drc-b06",
   "type": "example",
   "title": "$225^\\circ$ in radians",
   "problem": "Convert $225^\\circ$ to radians. Give an exact answer.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "md": "Multiply: $225^\\circ\\cdot\\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ}=\\dfrac{225\\pi}{180}$."
    },
    {
     "md": "Divide numerator and denominator by $45$: $\\dfrac{225}{180}=\\dfrac{5}{4}$.",
     "note": "Leave $\\pi$ in the answer: $3.93$ is an approximation, not the exact value."
    },
    {
     "md": "So $225^\\circ=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}$."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$\\dfrac{5\\pi}{4}$"
  },
  {
   "id": "drc-b07",
   "type": "example",
   "title": "$\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}$ in degrees",
   "problem": "Convert $\\dfrac{7\\pi}{6}$ to degrees.",
   "steps": [
    {
     "md": "Replace $\\pi$ with $180^\\circ$: $\\dfrac{7\\cdot180^\\circ}{6}$."
    },
    {
     "md": "$=7\\cdot30^\\circ=210^\\circ$."
    }
   ],
   "answer": "$210^\\circ$"
  },
  {
